---
// D429 — bespoke in-kernel reconstruction.
//
// The page IS the recreation: a document sheet centred on the ground, ruled by
// one axis — the running-text column's left edge. Masthead and body hang off
// that axis; a step-smaller note column hangs left of it in the margin, each
// entry on a hanging indent, with the artwork parked at the foot of the margin.
// The mechanic here IS the grid — the source has no interaction, so the page is
// static. The old build proved "the system runs deeper than the viewport" by
// CLIPPING at the fold behind a fixed viewport-height ceiling; that is the trap
// this change retires. It now proves the same thing honestly — the document runs
// down the page and you scroll it, a document not a website, exactly as the
// study notes claimed. De-branded: the source masthead becomes the work title,
// its line-drawing doodle becomes the committed photo parked in the margin, and
// the sheet's yellow ground drops to paper tones (the entry carries no palette,
// so the colour law holds).
//
// Reference (internal, never shipped): refs/works/d429.jpg, 1600×1000, ground
// L=255. Measured with scripts/ref-geometry.py + per-glyph probes, then
// converted x/1600*100 → cqw. Type is sized by CAP HEIGHT, not by eye: Archivo's
// cap is 0.686em (checked in the woff2, not assumed), so font-size = capH/0.686.
//
//   sheet        x315→1283 (969), centred, top y43   → 60.56cqw col, 2.69cqw top
//   sheet pad    left 43, right 43 (1240→1283)       → padding-inline 2.69cqw
//   note column  x358→626 (268 of the 883 content)   → 268fr
//   gutter       268→658, a 32px wall                → column-gap 3.62%
//   body column  x658→1240 (582) — THE axis          → 583fr
//   masthead     capH 36 (y95→130)                   → 3.28cqw  (36/.686 = 52.5px)
//   sub          capH 19 (y158→176), 10px under it   → 1.73cqw, margin-top .64cqw
//   deep band    sub baseline 176 → body cap top 346 → margin-bottom 9.77cqw
//   body         capH 14, line pitch 25px            → 1.275cqw / line-height 1.225
//   notes        capH 11, line pitch 19.2px          → 1cqw / line-height 1.2
//   note hang    line 1 x358, line 2 x388 (30px)     → 1.9em hanging indent
//   para indent  column 658 → 'Lorem' at 701 (34px)  → text-indent 1.65em
//   lead break   only ONE blank line (y471→522), the → .body__lead margin 1lh;
//                rest of the paragraphs indent only     no gap between the rest
//   figure       ink x366→613, y678→945; notes end   → margin-top 3.08cqw,
//                628, so a 268-square at the foot       aspect-ratio 1
//
// The reference's serif is not ours (the kernel ships two families, neither a
// serif), so the body is Archivo matched on cap height + line pitch — the two
// measurable things — and the glyph-width delta is accepted, per the method.
// The sheet's yellow is likewise the client's, not ours to keep.
//
// Archived case-study copy (removed from DOM 2026-07-17):
//
//   The signature —
//   The page is a sheet, not a screen: one document block centred against the
//   ground and cropped mid-sentence at the fold. Everything obeys a single axis —
//   the masthead and the running serif text share one left edge, while the
//   marginal notes hang to its left, set smaller, each entry indenting its own
//   continuation lines. A deep band of nothing between masthead and text does the
//   pacing; the drawing sits in the margin like a footnote.
//
//   Study notes —
//   The spacing works because the hierarchy is spatial before it is typographic:
//   primary text owns the axis, secondary text hangs off it, and rank is legible
//   from position alone before a single size difference registers. The oversized
//   band under the masthead is the one extravagance, and it is load-bearing — it
//   separates the document's name from the document's body the way a title page
//   separates itself from chapter one. Rebuilding it meant transcribing three
//   measures — note column, gutter, text column — and letting every other
//   decision fall out of them.
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('works', 'd429');
const w = entry!.data;

// The margin column — a second, subordinate voice. Italic entries echo the
// source's asides; the grid never moves for them. Four entries, one italic:
// the reference's own count. Copy is lorem; the structure is the point.
const notes = [
  { text: 'Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit sed do eiusmod.' },
  { text: 'Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris.', em: true },
  { text: 'Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum.' },
  { text: 'Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia.' },
];
---

<Base title={`${w.title} — *blue red + purple/`} chrome="work">
  <main class="site">
    <div class="sheet">
      <div class="sheet__grid">
        <header class="masthead">
          <p class="masthead__title ttu">{w.title} Study Doc</p>
          <p class="masthead__sub ttu">{w.category} · {w.year}</p>
        </header>

        <aside class="margin">
          <ul class="margin__notes">
            {notes.map((n) => <li class:list={{ em: n.em }}>{n.text}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <figure class="margin__plate">
            <img
              src={w.image}
              alt={`${w.title} plate`}
              width="536"
              height="536"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </figure>
        </aside>

        <div class="body">
          <p class="body__lead">
            <em>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet,</em> consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor
            incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud
            exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat.
          </p>
          <p>
            Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat
            nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, <em>sunt in culpa qui
            officia deserunt</em> mollit anim id est laborum et dolorum fuga.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sed ut perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque
            laudantium, totam rem aperiam, eaque ipsa quae ab illo inventore veritatis et quasi
            architecto beatae vitae dicta sunt explicabo, nemo enim ipsam voluptatem.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Neque porro quisquam est,</em> qui dolorem ipsum quia dolor sit amet, consectetur,
            adipisci velit, sed quia non numquam eius modi tempora incidunt ut labore et dolore
            magnam aliquam quaerat voluptatem, ut enim ad minima veniam quis nostrum.
          </p>
          <p>
            Quis autem vel eum iure reprehenderit qui in ea voluptate velit esse quam nihil
            molestiae consequatur, vel illum qui dolorem eum fugiat quo voluptas nulla pariatur, at
            vero eos et accusamus et iusto odio dignissimos ducimus qui blanditiis.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The rebuilt room, now the whole page. The sheet is centred and starts 43px
     off the top edge, then runs DOWN the page — the document genuinely runs
     deeper than the viewport and you scroll it (the old fixed-viewport clip is
     retired; min-height lets the sheet grow with its content). Static by
     design: the source has no mechanic, the grid is the mechanic.

     Top clearance opts into the shared chrome band via max(): the sheet's own
     2.69cqw inset holds on wide screens, but never less than --chrome-band, so
     the masthead's first line clears the pinned ← Index at every width (the
     narrow-screen collision the sweep flagged is gone).

     The page is its own query container. Every cqw below sits on a DESCENDANT of
     it and resolves against the viewport (1600) rather than the old ~1000px
     stage, which is the whole point. */
  .site {
    container-type: inline-size;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 60.56cqw;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-top: max(2.69cqw, var(--chrome-band));
    min-height: 100svh;
    background-color: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Sheet ink starts 43px in on both sides; the bottom border is omitted
     because the reference has no bottom — it is cropped. */
  .sheet {
    padding: 2.69cqw 2.69cqw 0;
    background-color: var(--paper-1, var(--paper));
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  /* One grid carries the whole sheet: notes 268 / gutter 32 / text 583 of the
     883px content box. The masthead spans only the text column — that is the
     axis rule, stated as geometry rather than as alignment. */
  .sheet__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 268fr 583fr;
    grid-template-areas:
      '.      masthead'
      'margin body';
    column-gap: 3.62%;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .masthead {
    grid-area: masthead;
    margin-bottom: 9.77cqw; /* the deep band between name and body: 156px */
  }
  .masthead__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--wght-display, 700);
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.28cqw, 4.1rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display, -0.02em);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .masthead__sub {
    margin: 0.64cqw 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(0.85rem, 1.73cqw, 2.16rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }

  .margin {
    grid-area: margin;
  }
  .margin__notes {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: clamp(0.75rem, 1cqw, 1.25rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }
  /* Hanging indent: first line flush on the note axis, continuation lines tucked
     30px behind it. Entries run on one continuous pitch — the reference puts no
     air between them, so the margin reads as a single ledger. */
  .margin__notes li {
    padding-left: 1.9em;
    text-indent: -1.9em;
  }
  .margin__notes li.em {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* Where the source parks its line drawing: now the committed photo itself, a
     square plate at the foot of the margin, spanning the column and standing as
     tall as it is wide (the reference's drawing is 268px in a 268px column). The
     paper-tint fill is the ghost skeleton — the image reserves its square and
     fades in over it on load (no JS — @starting-style), so the margin never
     shifts as the bytes arrive. */
  .margin__plate {
    margin: 3.08cqw 0 0;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    background-color: var(--paper-2, var(--paper-1, var(--paper)));
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
  }
  .margin__plate img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    opacity: 1;
    transition: opacity 0.6s var(--ease, ease);
  }
  @starting-style {
    .margin__plate img {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .margin__plate img {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .body {
    grid-area: body;
    font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1.275cqw, 1.6rem);
    line-height: 1.225; /* 25px pitch at the reference's 20.4px body */
  }
  .body p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  /* Source rhythm: an italic lead-in opens the block, exactly one blank line
     separates it from the running text, and every paragraph after that is
     indented with no air at all. The gap is one line, so state it as one line. */
  .body__lead {
    margin-bottom: 1lh;
  }
  .body__lead ~ p {
    text-indent: 1.65em;
  }

  /* Phone: the source sheet adapting, not a second design. The two columns are
     the work — collapsing them would break the axis the whole page is about — so
     the sheet widens instead and the note column takes the smallest share that
     still holds a measure. Type is already on rem floors above. */
  @container (max-width: 48rem) {
    .site {
      grid-template-columns: 94cqw;
    }
    .sheet__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 34fr 66fr;
    }
    .masthead {
      margin-bottom: 3.2rem;
    }
    .margin__plate {
      margin-top: 2rem;
    }
  }
</style>
